['```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <ctype.h>

int atoi(char* str) {
    int result = 0;
    int sign = 1;

    while (*str == ' ') ++str;

    if (*str == '-') {
        sign = -1;
        ++str;
    }
    else if (*str == '+') ++str;

    while (isdigit(*str)) {
        result = result * 10 + (*(str++) - '0');
    }

    return sign * result;
}

int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    if (argc < 3) {
        printf("Usage: %s index\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    int index = atoi(argv[2]);
    printf("%s\n", argv[index]);
    return 0;
}
```
This C code defines an `atoi()` function for converting a string to an integer and a `main()` function that takes command-line arguments, checks if the correct number of arguments are provided, finds the specified index using the second argument passed to the program, prints the corresponding argument at that index, and finally exits with status code 0.']

[' Here's a simple C code implementation for converting command line arguments from strings to integers using atoi(). This program takes advantage of the `argc` and `argv` parameters passed into the main function when you run a C program.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc <= 1) {
        printf("Usage: %s number\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    int number;
    char* endptr;

    // Convert the first argument to an integer using atoi().
    errno = 0;
    number = strtol(argv[1], &endptr, 10);

    if (errno == ERANGE || *endptr != '\0') {
        perror("Invalid number");
        return 2;
    }

    printf("The integer representation of the input is: %d\n", number);
    return 0;
}
```

This program will take one argument from the command line, convert it to an integer using atoi(), and print the result. If the provided string cannot be converted to an integer, it will show an error message.

You can compile this code with:

```sh
gcc main.c -o my_atoi
```

And run it like so:

```sh
./my_atoi 42
```']

